 "title": "Persistence needs forgetting: no finite substrate has an invertible step and a persistent contact",
 "subject": "Networks of rings joined by shared contacts, read with the canonical knot test on their space-time law",
 "conclusion": "On a finite network an invertible step returns every state to itself, and at that lag each contact holds only its own first value, bound to neither ring, so no contact persists. Hence wherever a contact persists no row after the first is open, for every rule and law, and growth while running cannot be rescued by an invertible substrate.",
